[Music]  my name is clinton jones and i love  creating atmospheric photoreal 3d  renders  this full cg environment took me six  weeks to complete and stretched my  ability as a 3d artist so i'd love to  share some new things that i learned  along the way as well as some old tricks  i usually incorporate to elevate my 3d  art regardless of your skill level or  program of choice i believe this  breakdown will have something for  everybody so let's begin with sparking  the idea  growing up i've always had a fascination  for abandoned buildings i find a  mysterious tranquility in exploring  these forgotten places  there's an endless supply of texture to  photograph and the way in which nature  takes over these man-made structures is  a beautiful sight to see  the on-edge feeling i have while  exploring these unpredictable places  piques my creativity in a unique way  as a 3d artist i've always wanted to  create one of these environments myself  but knew it would come with endless  challenges the amount of detail required  to genuinely portray this kind of  environment would strike fear in even  the most seasoned artists  this is going to take patience  perseverance and drive  so to guide my journey ahead i need to  get inspired and gather reference  [Music]  over the years i've collected various  art books featuring unique locations  from around the world  massive sewers from japan long forgotten  cities from china and vast stepwells in  india i use my art books as idea  starters and for this project in  particular one book stood out  just 10 miles off the coast of nagasaki  is a forgotten coal mining island  hashima or battleship island once home  to 5 000 miners and their families was  abandoned in 1974 and since then nature  has taken its toll the images in this  book have given me a closer idea of the  environment i want to aim for  in the supplement i've pulled  screenshots from my favorite movies for  style tone and color reference and  compiled them in a program called puref  at this point i'm ready to create a bit  of concept art utilizing the reference  imagery i've gathered  the process of creating concept art is  my time to test out ideas and  efficiently put the image i see in my  head onto a screen a single piece of  concept art will act as a guide toward  my version 1 render but only through  experimentation will i discover my final  piece keep an open mind as you create  and don't blind yourself with a single  idea  and with that it's time to build the  foreground which will require the most  detail  and real displacement textures will give  me the highest quality results to get  the most out of these textures i'm  stacking three planes each with their  own texture  one muddy ground one slick wet ground  and another broken brick texture and  i'll control which texture is visible  using a displacement modifier powered by  a cinema 4d noise texture this grants me  full control over how much of each  individual texture is visible i'll add a  large area light in the distance  pointing toward the camera in order to  get a bit more detail off the  reflections in the mud then block out  some buildings to get a better feel of  the scene to add a bit more detail to my  foreground i'm using the quixote mega  scans library to scatter appropriate  items like rusted pipes old bottles cans  and mining equipment across the ground  i'll also add a few rubble piles and a  rusted barrel to add more texture to the  scene as well as some areas of raised  mud in the corners to keep things a bit  more realistic but before i go any  further i have to warn you guys about  virtual ram management luckily i'm  running an nvidia rtx quadro 6000 but  even with 24 gigabytes of virtual ram  this scene managed to use most of it  first use low res textures first  and only replace them at the end of your  project if you can clearly tell they're  low res this way you don't have to  replace every single texture when your  system inevitably locks up  second is to ensure your black and white  textures are red as such as opposed to  color textures which take up more vram  and finally use instances when  duplicating geometry instances reference  a model versus duplicating 30 versions  of the same mesh it's the only reason  i'm able to populate my scene with these  extremely high-res photo scans and only  by sticking to these three ram saving  tips was i able to complete this  ambitious scene  and finally to wrap out the foreground i  added a large storm drain that trails  off into the background but ran into an  issue when i realized there weren't any  storm drain textures online yeah there's  concrete textures but naturally storm  drains are made from endless slabs of  concrete that over time have collected  layer upon layer of unique texture  the two endless strips of concrete i saw  it were hidden underneath a massive  bridge  and inside an actual storm drain  for both i captured a series of images  with my iphone making sure to include  about 40 overlap between each shot in  lightroom i made batch edits to correct  for distortion perspective and aspect  ratio and stitched them together in  photoshop once my massive color map was  created i generated a normal map which  adds surface detail and a roughness and  specular map to decide how shiny our  concrete is and pretty soon here i'll be  releasing a start to finish in depth  video on how you can venture out take  some high quality textures and process  everything yourself so be sure to  subscribe to stay in the know this video  is brought to you by monk manual it's a  90-day planner designed to bring the  timeless wisdom of monk livin to your  everyday life i'm a true believer in the  idea that consistency and reflection is  the only way to truly master something  and the monk manual is specifically  designed to guide you through that  process  most planners only have space for your  schedule but the monk manual goes so  much further its daily pages are the  meat of the planner and help you  schedule your most important tasks track  habits you're working on and most  importantly reflect on your day's  progress and the weekly and monthly  pages allow you to zoom out and track  your progress over a longer period of  time in order to truly see how you're  doing so if you're trying to take on a  new challenge learn a new skill or  simply want to fill your life with a bit  more happiness the monk manual is for  you i've been using it for a while now  and couldn't recommend it more click the  link below use the code punisher that's  p-w-n-i-s-h-e-r  to get 10 off any order and start  tracking your growth today  so now that we've made a custom storm  drain let me show you guys how i created  my custom character  i wanted something a bit more unique  than your average miximo character for  the surrender so i pieced together a  costume half motorcycle gear half rock  climbing equipment and take a bunch of  photos from every angle in an area with  no directional light one pass at chest  level one pass above the head and  another super low will do the trick in  this case  i processed my images in a program  called reality capture which generates a  detailed 3d mesh from the series of  images since the photo scan comes at  such a high poly count i derez the mesh  in c4d export in fbx and auto rig it  with miximo adobe's free motion capture  library from here i'll re-export as a  t-pose back into c4d and now i have my  custom character rigged and ready for  animation and to keep things custom i'll  control my character with a rococo  motion capture suit i looped the walk  cycle in rococo studio check out this  video right here if you guys want to  learn how i added my character to this  custom mocap data  at this point our render is looking nice  but i'm just not happy with it i need to  add a story to this piece and only by  adding story will this overplayed genre  have any chance of becoming unique so  let's clearly define our character by  adding a few character specific items  like carabiners a bundle of rope and  some more rock climbing equipment next  i'll add an old robot into our storm  drain i'll add a machine gun nest to  insinuate a war between man and machine  now not that original but hey it's  better than nothing and some 50 cow  shells scattered across the mud for some  added detail  and already this is better than no story  where does it take you guys  leave a comment down below  before we hop into after effects to add  animated atmosphere in a cinematic color  grade we need to fine-tune what i think  is the most important step  i'm talking about lighting only by  fine-tuning your lights in the absence  of light can we achieve stunning results  currently our scene is lit by a single  grey image and a simple area light to  catch detail in our ground reflections  the only other light i'll add is the sun  but now there's a problem because we've  lost our dark dramatic mood we've cast  light over the entire landscape so i'll  need to block light with specifically  placed non-reflective black planes  called flags this is exactly what would  happen on a real world movie set my goal  is to only illuminate the story elements  creating a more dramatic scene so let's  begin by placing a flag above the camera  in order to cast a shadow across our  foreground this naturally vignettes the  landscape i'll add another flag to the  area behind our subject naturally  spotlighting our character and i'll  place one last flag to highlight the old  robot and machine gun nest believe it or  not this process took me about a day or  two to get right so don't be discouraged  if the fine tuning takes longer than you  think i promise it'll be worth it  and before we render an image sequence  i'll make sure to cue up a z depth pass  which is crucial for adding animated  smoke in after effects but let's start  with a cinematic color grade i always  begin with my shadows mid-tones and  highlights also known as lift gamma and  gain locking these light values in first  ensures that you can see the detail in  the darkest shadows and the brightest  highlights before tweaking color once  things look nice i'll push my shadows to  blue and bring out the green for a nice  complementary color boost i'll also  bring a bit more warmth into the  highlights and give the entire image a  saturation boost finally let's add a  subtle vignette and now we have a nice  cinematic color grade  currently our character is blending in  with the background so we need to find a  creative way to add contrast there's a  site called action vfx that not only has  the largest selection of stock footage  elements but their stock footage is the  highest quality as well i'll use their  atmospheric smoke and fog pack to bring  my render to life and draw attention to  specific elements by increasing contrast  in specific areas  this will take our render to the next  level  and finally after adding some subtle  film grain to dirty up our render and a  sound design pass to round things out  our art is complete  just remember if you guys can picture it  you can create it  thanks for watching everybody  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  you

